"The cause is in \u0060OrderApi.GetOrderTotal\u0060 at \u0060src/OrderService/Api/OrderTotal.cs\u0060 line 18, where \u0060DiscountRates[order.DiscountCode]\u0060 is an unguarded indexer lookup. I\u0027m highly confident this is a code issue. I changed no files.\n\n- **SUMMER25 is absent from the code\u0027s discount table.** \u0060DiscountRates\u0060 contains only \u0060WELCOME10\u0060 (0.10) and \u0060VIP20\u0060 (0.20). A repository-wide search for \u0060SUMMER25\u0060 finds no match. Order 1003 in \u0060data/orders.csv\u0060 carries \u0060SUMMER25\u0060, a retired code the runbook says can appear.\n- **Line 18 is the only unguarded discount lookup.** \u0060DiscountRates\u0060 is indexed in only that one place. Two other paths touch the order\u0027s code, but neither is a discount-table lookup that can throw this exception. \u0060GetDiscountLabel\u0060 parses the code\u0027s last two characters rather than using a table. \u0060GetVat\u0060 does its own unguarded lookup, but into \u0060VatRates\u0060 keyed by country, and it calls \u0060GetOrderTotal\u0060, so \u0060vat\u0060 for order 1003 would fail the same way.\n- **Expected behaviour.** The runbook says an unknown or retired code should give no discount, so the total should equal the subtotal. The fix would be a guarded lookup such as \u0060TryGetValue\u0060, falling back to a rate of zero. Totals for WELCOME10, VIP20 and no-code orders would not change.\n\nI did not run the test suite. The conclusion rests on reading the code and data.\n\n[Answered by triage.investigator, did: ReadWorkspaceFile: namespace OrderService;; ReadWorkspaceFile: using System.Globalization;; SearchWorkspace: No matches found.; SearchWorkspace: demos/louis-agent/samples/order-service/src/OrderService/Api/OrderTotal.cs:5: p\u2026; SearchWorkspace: demos/louis-agent/samples/order-service/tests/OrderService.Tests/OrderApiTests.\u2026; FindFiles: Found 22 file(s) matching \u0027*.cs\u0027:; ReadWorkspaceFile: id,customer,items,discount_c